178 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(WHAT ARE MOE'S AND LLMS?)/E(WHAT ARE MOE'S AND LLMS?)/Pg 11 0 R/K[7]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

179 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(AI languages are getting more powerful. Large language models \(LLMs\) and Mixture of Experts \(MoEs\) are the two most utilised architectures that dominate the field. While both are used in most AI models, they're not used equally and operate differently. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[0]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

180 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(LLMS)/E(LLMS)/Pg 11 0 R/K[6]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

181 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Large language models are AI systems trained on massive amounts of text to understand data. Due to their generalisation ability, they can handle various tasks and are well-understood in the industry. However, they use every part of their neural network every time they process information, increasing the cost and consuming huge amounts of power even for smaller tasks.  )/Pg 11 0 R/K[4]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

182 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(This is primarily seen in OpenAI's GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3 and most traditional Qwen models. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[5]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

183 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(MOES)/E(MOES)/Pg 11 0 R/K[8]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

184 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(A mixture of Experts separates large AI models into smaller networks, each handling their own tasks; instead of using a whole model at once, MoEs only use specific 'experts' depending on the task, saving huge amounts of computing power and more efficiently using its resources. However, the downsides include a lack of stability and consistency in responses since different agents are activated at other times; the complexity of training this model thus increases. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[9]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

185 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(As a quick analogy, LLMs are more like an encyclopedia, while a MoE is more like a group of experts who are harder to organise but, once trained correctly, are much more efficient. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[10]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

186 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(This is primarily seen in OpenAI's GPT-4o, DeepSeek-V3 and most traditional Qwen models. )/Pg 11 0 R/K[11]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

187 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(A BRIEF HISTORY)/E(A BRIEF HISTORY)/Pg 11 0 R/K[13]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

188 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Since 2023, Alibaba\220s Qwen series has aimed to dominate China\220s AI market, evolving from its first chatbot \(Qwen-1\) to the latest Qwen 2.5-Max, which claims to surpass DeepSeek-V3 and GPT-4o. Major milestones include the introduction of open-source models \(Qwen-1.5\), the shift to Mixture-of-Experts \(Qwen-2\), and the launch of specialized AI like Qwen-VL \(vision-language\), Qwen-Audio \(speech processing\), and Qwen2-Math \(advanced calculations\).)/Pg 11 0 R/K[12]/P 98 0 R>>
endobj

