361 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(USE ISN\220T THE PROBLEM; TRAINING IS. )/E(USE ISN\220T THE PROBLEM; TRAINING IS. )/Pg 21 0 R/K[8]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

362 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(The real energy draining isn\220t on individual AI queries but the initial training of AI-models. GPT-3\220s training alone consumed energy estimated to be around 1.3 gigawatt-hours, energy levels equivalent to 120 average houses in the US being powered for an entire year. Tech firms rarely disclose precise energy consumption figures so its left to estimation through independent researches to estimate costs, relying primarily on hardware shipments and third party data. Although not all data centres are dedicated to AI, AI training takes place in data centres, models are trained on high performance clusters on GPUs and data centres demand will get higher and higher as need for AI training increases. Ireland for example has shown that data centres already account for nearly 20% of the countries electricity use, studies suggest the AI industry worldwide could match the energy consumed by the Netherlands by 2027. )/Pg 21 0 R/K[3]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

363 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(If GPT-4 required even more power than GPT-3, what about GPT-5, GPT-6, or the industry as a whole in 2030, will current supply meet demand?)/Pg 21 0 R/K[4]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

364 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/H1/T(SO WHATS THE BIG DEAL?)/E(SO WHATS THE BIG DEAL?)/Pg 21 0 R/K[2]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

365 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(As previously mentioned, with strains on water and energy supply for upcoming data centres not just in the uk but worldwide as countries adapt from France to China to Japan adapt to an increase in AI demand, there will be an increasing need for AI. AI being used in increasingly more industries; games, vehicles, customer support or even daily use can add up, even on a seemingly minute scale. The growth for AI if not continuously utilised could put an increase demand on energy grids and water supply. )/Pg 21 0 R/K[9]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

366 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(With these issues, there remains a greater push towards green data centres which optimise physical design for efficiency airflow, advanced cooling methods and integration of renewable energy sources, reducing building footprint. Similar with AI, there is a benefit to companies through a long-term reductional in operational costs although the higher initial investment can be seen as a risk to some. )/Pg 21 0 R/K[10]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

367 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/K[11]/Pg 21 0 R/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

368 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/E(Can AI companies and governments keep up with AI\220s energy demands, or will the power grid become the next bottleneck?)/Pg 21 0 R/K[12]/P 108 0 R>>
endobj

